---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/data-coverage/');

const coverage = [
  {
    area: 'Quantity and category samples',
    keeps: 'Values, units, dates, source, device, metadata, and deletion tombstones.',
    limits:
      'Hozz asks for the types it reads. Where Health cannot distinguish denied from empty, Hozz says so rather than guessing.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Workouts',
    keeps:
      'Activity type, duration, events, and Health’s own aggregate statistics, including per-leg figures for multi-sport workouts.',
    limits: 'These are summaries of samples exported separately, not duplicate samples.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Workout routes',
    keeps:
      'The route sample, the confirmed workout relationship when Health can prove it, 500-point location pages, and an end marker with the final point count.',
    limits: 'Hozz does not guess a workout from time overlap alone. Unresolved routes say why.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Electrocardiograms',
    keeps:
      'Classification, raw classification value, symptoms status, average heart rate, sampling frequency, expected waveform count, and 500-reading voltage pages.',
    limits:
      'Missing voltage readings stay gaps, not zeros, and a partial waveform is never presented as a whole one.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Audiograms',
    keeps:
      'One row per hearing threshold, with ear, frequency, sensitivity, clamping bounds, and where supported, conduction and masking.',
    limits: 'An unmeasured ear is absent, not written as 0 dBHL.',
  },
  {
    area: 'State of Mind',
    keeps:
      'Valence, Health’s classification, momentary emotion versus daily mood, labels, associations, and raw enum values.',
    limits:
      'iOS 18+. Zero valence is neutral, not missing. An empty label list means none were chosen.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Medication doses',
    keeps:
      'Dose status, schedule type, optional dose quantities, medication resolution state, form, nickname, archived and schedule flags, and codings.',
    limits:
      'iOS 26+. This is the dose-logging API, not clinical medication records. Only “taken” means taken.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Health characteristics',
    keeps:
      'Date of birth, biological sex, blood type, Fitzpatrick skin type, wheelchair use, and activity move mode, each with a state.',
    limits:
      'These are facts about the person, not samples. They have no UUID, source, dates, or anchor.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Quantity series',
    keeps:
      'The aggregate sample, with its count, marked as aggregating a series when it stands for more than one reading.',
    limits: 'Hozz does not expand quantity series into individual readings yet.',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="The data">
  <p>
    Hozz is early alpha and its coverage is partial. This page says what is covered and what is not,
    because the alternative — implying completeness and letting you discover the gaps — is the thing
    an archive tool can least afford to do.
  </p>

  <h2>What Hozz exports today</h2>

  <p>
    Quantity samples, category samples, workout records and Health's workout statistics, paged
    workout routes, electrocardiograms with voltage waveforms, audiograms, State of Mind entries,
    medication dose events, historical deletions, and the six Health characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Correlations, documents, scored assessments and most other series are <strong>catalogued or
      reported as unsupported</strong>, rather than silently claimed as coverage.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Health data areas, what Hozz keeps, and the limits on each"
    columns={['Area', 'What Hozz keeps', 'Important limits']}
  >
    {
      coverage.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.area}</th>
          <td>{row.keeps}</td>
          <td>{row.limits}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <h2>The join Hozz refuses to make up</h2>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Hozz does not export which samples belonged to a workout">
    <p>
      HealthKit will list the objects belonging to a workout, but offers nothing in the other
      direction — a sample does not know its workout, and no workout identifier appears in its
      metadata. Recording the link would mean a query per workout per type: tens of thousands of
      them for a real history.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Matching by time range is therefore an approximation, and worth knowing you are making one. It
    is a good approximation for most workouts, and wrong in two situations: <strong>workouts that
      overlap in time</strong>, and <strong>samples recorded inside the window by something other
      than the workout</strong> — a phone counting steps in a pocket during a ride, for instance.
  </p>

  <p>
    The workout's own statistics are exact, because Health computed them. A time-range join is your
    reconstruction, not Health's answer. Both are useful; they are not the same thing, and only one
    of them is a fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Denied and empty look identical</h2>

  <p>
    Apple does not let apps distinguish "the user denied this Health type" from "there is no
    matching data". That is a deliberate privacy decision — telling an app would leak the existence
    of data you chose to withhold.
  </p>

  <p>
    So Hozz reports authorization-scoped coverage, and keeps denied-or-empty, unavailable,
    unsupported and failed as distinct visible states rather than flattening them into a number. A
    type with no records says so; it does not claim you have none.
  </p>

  <h2>Health records (clinical records)</h2>

  <p>
    Clinical records are FHIR resources from a connected provider: lab results, conditions, clinical
    medications, immunisations, procedures, vital signs, coverage records, allergies and notes.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Built, and switched off in every default build">
    <p>
      The code that would read them is <strong>compiled out</strong>. Reading them needs the{' '}
      <code>com.apple.developer.healthkit.access</code> entitlement with <code>health-records</code>.
      The entitlement array ships empty and the Swift flag that enables the code is undefined by
      default, so shipping them stays a deliberate decision rather than a default.
    </p>
    <p>
      When it is off — which is every default build today — the app says health records are{' '}
      <strong>not available in this build</strong>, and says explicitly that this is not a statement
      about whether you have any.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>Turning them on takes two separate, deliberate steps: a build flag, and a visible edit to a
    tracked file that changes the entitlement. They are separate switches on purpose, so that
    enabling one without the other fails loudly rather than becoming a quietly disabled feature.</p>

  <p>Three things about the data itself, if it is ever enabled:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Consent is per record.</strong> Health asks which records to share one by one, and
      Hozz cannot see what was withheld. Partial access is the normal case, not a failure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The FHIR resource is carried through as the provider sent it</strong>, not reshaped
      into Hozz's vocabulary. A result's units, reference ranges and coding systems <em>are</em> the
      clinical meaning. A resource Hozz cannot parse is kept as bytes rather than dropped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A clinical record's identity is not its HealthKit UUID.</strong> Apple says that UUID
      is not stable for these records, so identity is derived from source, resource type and FHIR
      identifier, with the UUID kept alongside and labelled for tracing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-hozz-will-never-do">What Hozz will never do</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Hozz will never write anything back into Apple Health.</strong> That is a decision, not
    an oversight, and not something waiting on time.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Health permanently attributes every sample to the app that wrote it. Anything Hozz imported
      would be stamped as Hozz's data forever, so a heart rate your Watch recorded in 2019 and Hozz
      restored in 2027 would be indistinguishable from one Hozz invented. That destroys exactly the
      provenance an archive exists to preserve.
    </li>
    <li>
      HealthKit has no upsert. There is no way to say "store this unless it is already there", so
      importing the same export twice silently doubles it, with no reliable way afterwards to tell
      the copies apart. A tool whose whole argument is that it never loses or duplicates a record
      cannot ship an operation that duplicates records by design.
    </li>
    <li>
      It could not be complete anyway: characteristics and clinical records cannot be written by an
      app at all, so even a perfect importer would restore some of your data and quietly skip the
      rest.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not yet</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Every Health type — coverage is partial, and this page is the current boundary.</li>
    <li>Expanding quantity series into individual readings.</li>
    <li>Clinical records in a shipped build.</li>
    <li>Handover between two devices.</li>
    <li>An accessibility and localisation pass.</li>
    <li>An App Store release.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
